# The folder tree's selection, which used to be lost every time its rows were rebuilt.
#
# Both cases below go through Children.Clear(), and WPF answers the removal of the selected
# TreeViewItem by selecting its parent — which raised DirectorySelected and navigated the tab
# to the drive root. The `settle` calls are load-bearing: the selection fix-up happens on the
# next layout pass, so an assertion made immediately after the command passes either way.
#
# Run against a sandbox at exactly this path — the tree-click assertions name it, and it has to
# be somewhere visible rather than the default under %TEMP%, since AppData is hidden and with
# "Show hidden items" off the tree cannot reveal down to it at all:
#   --sandbox C:\Source\bertbrowser-treecheck --allow-outside

tree .
refresh
go Documents
settle 400
assert-path Documents

# Toggling "Show hidden items" re-filters every node in the tree.
hidden on
settle 500
assert-path Documents
shot 01-hidden-on
hidden off
settle 500
assert-path Documents
hidden on
settle 500
assert-path Documents
hidden off
settle 500
assert-path Documents

# A transfer repopulates the affected folders, which builds new child nodes — so the selected
# row is not merely re-filtered, it is replaced.
select letter.txt
move to .
settle 500
assert-path Documents
shot 02-after-move
undo
settle 500
assert-path Documents

# A delete refreshes the same way.
select draft.md
delete
settle 500
assert-path Documents
undo
settle 500
assert-path Documents
assert-row draft.md

# The other half of the same rule: none of the above may have cost the tree its ability to
# navigate when a person really does click a row. The guards work by ignoring selections the
# tree made itself, so this is what stops them ignoring one it did not.
tree-click .
assert-path bertbrowser-treecheck
tree-click Documents
assert-path Documents
tree-click Empty
assert-path Empty
hidden on
settle 500
tree-click .
assert-path bertbrowser-treecheck
tree-click Pictures
assert-path Pictures
hidden off
settle 500

state
